[1mNAME[0m
       MAX, MIN - maximum or minimum of two values

[1mLIBRARY[0m
       Standard C library ([4mlibc[24m, [4m-lc[24m)

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
       [1m#include <sys/param.h>[0m

       [1mMAX([4m[22ma[24m[1m, [4m[22mb[24m[1m);[0m
       [1mMIN([4m[22ma[24m[1m, [4m[22mb[24m[1m);[0m

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       These macros return the maximum or minimum of [4ma[24m and [4mb[24m.

[1mRETURN VALUE[0m
       These  macros  return  the  value of one of their arguments, possibly converted to a different
       type (see BUGS).

[1mERRORS[0m
       These macros may raise the "invalid" floating-point exception when any  of  the  arguments  is
       NaN.

[1mSTANDARDS[0m
       GNU, BSD.

[1mNOTES[0m
       If  either  of  the  arguments is of a floating-point type, you might prefer to use [1mfmax[22m(3) or
       [1mfmin[22m(3), which can handle NaN.

       The arguments may be evaluated more than once, or not at all.

       Some UNIX systems might provide these macros in a different header, or not at all.

[1mBUGS[0m
       Due to the usual arithmetic conversions, the result of these macros may be very different from
       either of the arguments.  To avoid this, ensure that both arguments have the same type.

[1mEXAMPLES[0m
       #include <stdio.h>
       #include <stdlib.h>
       #include <sys/param.h>

       int
       main(int argc, char *argv[])
       {
           int a, b, x;

           if (argc != 3) {
               fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <num> <num>\n", argv[0]);
               exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
           }

           a = atoi(argv[1]);
           b = atoi(argv[2]);
           x = MAX(a, b);
           printf("MAX(%d, %d) is %d\n", a, b, x);

           exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
       }

